	#tag Note, Name = Joules Calculation
		
		Descent Energy Calculation
		The brake pads in the DEUS 7300 are designed to absorb 200 million joules of energy. 
		Inspection intervals are every 15 million joules. 
		If someone in your organization is responsible for equipment inspection contact 3M for this training.
		Calculation of descent energy is done as follows:
		Multiply descent height (meters) times load (kilograms) times number of descents times 10. 
		( The last “10” refers to the acceleration constant of gravity. 
		The actual acceleration constant of gravity is 9.8 meters/sec/sec. 
		The value of 10 is used for simpler mental approximation. 9.8 can be used in your usage log for greater accuracy.) 
		The unit of measure of the resulting calculation is joules.
		Example:
		Assume an average weight per person of 220 lbs (100 kg), 
		an average descent distance of 33 ft (10 meters) and 1,500 descents of this load and distance.
		100 kg x 10 meters x 1,500 descents x 10 = 15,000,000 joules
		
		For us:
		
		Average Weight: 85 kg (187 lb)
		Average Descent: 23 Meters (75 ft)
		Acceleration of gravity: 10 m/sec2
		
		85 x 23 x 10 x Drops
		Euleer pins need to be replaced or spun at 24 mm diameter
		Brakes  need to be replaced at 200 M joules
	#tag EndNote


